msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:80
msgid "The style bar sits at the **top** of the video preview. If that would cover the text you are editing, it jumps to the **bottom** of the preview instead, and it returns to the top once the text is clear of it again (for example, after you move the text or make it smaller). The style bar floats over the video, so it never resizes the preview. Changes from the style bar show on the video right away."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:89
msgid "Edit Style"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:91
msgid "**Edit Style** has every text setting in one place. **Text** holds the words. **Font** has the style bar controls, the text outline (color and width), and letter and line spacing. **Background** has the box behind the text: its color, padding, rounded corners, border (color and width), and box width (where lines wrap). Open it from **More (…)** on the style bar, from **Text → Edit Style…** on a clip's context menu, or with **Edit Text…** in Project Files. The preview at the top shows changes as you make them. Press :kbd:`Ctrl+Enter` or click **Apply** to keep them; :kbd:`Enter` adds a new line, and **Cancel** discards the changes. **Restore Defaults** returns everything except the text to the default style."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:100
msgid "**Line spacing** sets the distance between lines as a multiple of the normal distance: 1.0 is normal, 1.5 adds half a line of space, and values below 1.0 pull lines closer together. **Corner radius** rounds the corners of the background and the border, in pixels; 0 keeps them square. The text outline is drawn behind the letters, so a wide outline grows outward instead of covering them. For hollow letters, give the text an outline and set the text color's opacity to 0 in the color picker: only the outline is drawn, and the video shows through the letters."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:107
msgid "Select several text clips (or several texts in Project Files) and choose **Edit Style…** to change their style together. The text box is grayed out, because each text keeps its own words, and only the settings you change are applied to all of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:111
msgid "To reset text clips without opening the dialog, right-click them and choose **Text → Reset Style**. Their font, size, colors and outlines return to the defaults, any color or outline keyframes on those clips are removed, and their words are kept."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:115
msgid "To give other text clips the same look, right-click a text clip and choose **Copy → Text Style**, then select the other text clips, right-click and choose **Paste**. They take the font, size, colors, outline, border, corners and spacing, plus the copied clip's color, outline, border and corner keyframes, and keep their own words and box width. Pasting a text style onto other kinds of clips changes nothing. **Copy → Keyframes → All** also includes a text clip's own text keyframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:122
msgid "A new text starts with the style you used last (font, size, colors, outline, border and spacing), so a series of titles stays consistent. Without a previous text, it uses a standard font installed on your computer."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:127
msgid "Project Files and Properties"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:129
msgid "Each text also appears in **Project Files**, with a thumbnail on black. Drag it onto the timeline like an image to use it again. Right-click it and choose **Edit Text…** (or double-click it) to edit it without placing it on the timeline. **Duplicate** opens a copy, ready to type, and **Apply** adds it as a new, independent text."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:134
msgid "The words, font, size, spacing, alignment, box width, padding and default colors belong to the text in Project Files, so editing them updates every clip that uses that text. When a text clip is selected, these settings also appear at the top of **Properties** (for example **Text Box Width** and **Text Background Padding**), and changes there show on the video right away (including while you are typing)."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:140
msgid "Text color, background, outline and border colors, outline and border widths, and corner radius can be animated on each clip with keyframes in **Properties**, like any other clip property. These follow **Auto Keyframes** at the playhead. Font size, padding, box width, and letter and line spacing set the size of the text box, so they have one value per text; to animate the size of text, animate the clip's scale. Undo and Redo work for all text changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:146
msgid "Text is saved in the project file and drawn by OpenShot's video library, so it needs no image files. Fonts come from your computer: to see the same text on another computer, install the same fonts there. One style applies to all of a text's lines."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:150
msgid "Text can also be added and styled from the command line; see :ref:`cli_text_ref`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/titles.rst:152
msgid "The **Title** editor (SVG templates and animated titles) and **Captions** (timed subtitles) remain available for more elaborate designs."
msgstr ""

